# Design Plan, the loop it was designed as. See `bug.json` beside this file for
# why a reduced form exists at all, and for the six fields this one also holds
# to.
#
# `crates/core-model/domain/workflows.toml` calls this Armada's only
# instantiated loop: `draft` and `present`, with `present`'s `request_changes`
# routing back to `draft`. It said so while this file could not carry it —
# `structure: loop` was outside M1's carried set, so this form kept both steps
# and dropped the loop. It carries it now. The designed
# `design-plan.json` (`crates/core-model/domain/workflow-samples/`) is what the
# routing and the cap of five are taken from; the cap lives on the step here
# rather than under `default_gate_policy`, which is still a deferred key.
#
# **`present` carries the designed `human_always`, and that is the half of the
# loop that always worked.** The thing that ends a design plan is a person
# reading it. What `request_changes` does with their answer is the half that
# arrived with the loop: it used to put the Drone back on `present`, which
# re-presented an unchanged document, and it now sends the work back to `draft`
# the way the design says.
#
# **A Job goes round once, and the second return has nowhere to land.** The
# loop return re-enters `draft` and leaves `present` reading `running`, which is
# how a step at a human gate already behaves and is what #263 settled — the
# alternatives were a seventh step state, which breaks the wire, and `stopped`,
# whose registry meaning is "retries spent". When the redrafted plan advances,
# Fleet walks forward onto `present` and finds it already `running`, and there
# is no self-edge. `crates/fleet/src/tests/looping.rs` pins the refusal by name.
# The cap stays at the designed five rather than being lowered to one to hide
# it: the bound is a property of the design, and the state the gate holds
# between passes is a property of the machine.
#
# **This workflow carries no Judge, and that is a decision rather than an
# omission.** The designed `design-plan.json` carries none either. Design Plan
# is a back-and-forth the engineer ends when the design is in a good place --
# `workflowdef-fields.toml` says treating that as a config error would break
# it. The thing that ends a design plan is a person reading it, and the loop
# that carries the reading back is what this file now declares.
#
# **A step whose product a later step reads writes a file.** `artifact_exists`
# is carried now, and `bug.json` carries the reasoning: a written step used to
# hand the next one three prose strings, two of which `verification::submission`
# says nothing routes on. Fleet opens the path this step declares and puts the
# file's contents in the Judge's brief as the document the step produced; the
# three strings are shown beside it, labelled as the summary. The mechanical
# check reads only the file's size, so an empty file stops the step and whether
# what is in it is any good stays the Judge's question.
#
# **`draft` is the one step where `artifact_exists` is the whole gate.** This
# workflow carries no Judge, so nothing else reads what `draft` produced --
# which is exactly why the check has to answer more than "a path exists". It
# refuses an empty file and it refuses a directory of that name, and it still
# cannot tell a considered design from a sentence. `present` is
# `human_always`, so the reader of the design is a person, which is what this
# workflow says it wants.
{
  "version": 1,
  "workflow_id": "design_plan",
  "name": "design_plan",
  "structure": "loop",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "draft",
      "label": "Draft",
      "evidence_type": "document",
      "mechanical_checks": [
        {
          "type": "artifact_exists",
          "target": ".armada/artifacts/draft.md"
        }
      ],
      "advance_gate": "auto"
    },
    {
      "id": "present",
      "label": "Present",
      "evidence_type": "document",
      "advance_gate": "human_always",
      "verdict_routing": {
        "request_changes": "draft"
      },
      "iteration_cap": 5
    }
  ]
}
